From 90af4fb7fd80e6f20dda035126530ce985e894fa Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: moWerk <mo@mowerk.net>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 00:04:43 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 2/4] firmware: qcom: tzmem: Return an errno when the SHM
 bridge call is refused
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

qcom_scm_shm_bridge_create() returns the firmware's own status when the
SMC went through but TZ refused the bridge, a positive number.
qcom_tzmem_shm_bridge_create() passes it on as is, and documents a
negative return. qcom_tzmem_pool_add_memory() and qcom_tzmem_pool_new()
pass it on too, the latter as ERR_PTR(): a positive value is not an
error pointer, so IS_ERR() clears it and the caller gets a pool pointer
with the numeric value of the firmware status. The first
qcom_tzmem_alloc() on that pool dereferences it.

Seen on an SM6115 phone (F(x)tec Pro1X) with the out-of-tree QSEECOM TEE
driver, which creates a pool per command: after a listener exchange that
TZ had been told failed, the next bridge creation came back with status
30 and the kernel oopsed in qcom_tzmem_alloc() at address 0x1e.

Map a firmware status to -EIO where the bridge is created, so every
caller sees the negative return the function promises.

Fixes: f86c61498a57 ("firmware: qcom: tzmem: enable SHM Bridge support")

Disclosure: LLMGD-2 · origin O1 (the model wrote it; the author tested the behaviour on the device and can verify that, not the code)
LLMGD: v0.3; assurance=A2; flags=T; origin=O1; origin_headline=O1; scope=kernel; graded-by=claude-fable-5-1; retrieval=none
---
 drivers/firmware/qcom/qcom_tzmem.c | 10 +++++++++-
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/firmware/qcom/qcom_tzmem.c b/drivers/firmware/qcom/qcom_tzmem.c
index f926c1f64..c94869751 100644
--- a/drivers/firmware/qcom/qcom_tzmem.c
+++ b/drivers/firmware/qcom/qcom_tzmem.c
@@ -143,7 +143,15 @@ int qcom_tzmem_shm_bridge_create(phys_addr_t paddr, size_t size, u64 *handle)
 			"SHM Bridge failed: ret %d paddr 0x%pa, size %zu\n",
 			ret, &paddr, size);
 
-		return ret;
+		/*
+		 * When the SMC itself went through, this is the firmware's
+		 * own status, a positive number. Callers feed the return
+		 * value to ERR_PTR(), and a positive value is not an error
+		 * pointer: qcom_tzmem_pool_new() would hand it out as a pool
+		 * and the first qcom_tzmem_alloc() on it dereferences a small
+		 * integer.
+		 */
+		return ret < 0 ? ret : -EIO;
 	}
 
 	return 0;
